[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Props: Passing Data and Immutability in React"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In React, props (short for properties) are the primary mechanism for passing data from a parent component to its child components. They are read-only, meaning a child component cannot directly modify the props it receives. This immutability is a core principle in React that helps maintain predictable data flow and simplifies debugging."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Props"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Props are similar to HTML attributes, but they are used to pass data to React components. They can be of any data type, including strings, numbers, arrays, objects, and even functions."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Passing Props"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To pass props to a component, you specify them as attributes when you render the component. The child component then receives these props as an object."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"function ParentComponent() {
  const message = "Hello from Parent!";
  const number = 42;
  const data = { name: "React", version: "18.0" };

  return (
    <div>
      <ChildComponent message={message} number={number} data={data} />
    </div>
  );
}

function ChildComponent(props) {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Message: {props.message}</p>
      <p>Number: {props.number}</p>
      <p>Data Name: {props.data.name}</p>
      <p>Data Version: {props.data.version}</p>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `ParentComponent` passes the `message`, `number`, and `data` props to `ChildComponent`. `ChildComponent` then accesses these props using `props.message`, `props.number`, and `props.data`."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Destructuring"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use destructuring to make your code more concise and readable when accessing props."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"function ChildComponent({ message, number, data }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Message: {message}</p>
      <p>Number: {number}</p>
      <p>Data Name: {data.name}</p>
      <p>Data Version: {data.version}</p>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"This is equivalent to the previous example but avoids having to write `props.` repeatedly."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Default Props"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define default values for props using `defaultProps`. This is useful when a prop might not always be provided by the parent component."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"function MyComponent({ name }) {
  return <p>Hello, {name}!</p>;
}

MyComponent.defaultProps = {
  name: "Guest",
};

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <MyComponent /> {/* Renders "Hello, Guest!" */}
      <MyComponent name="Alice" /> {/* Renders "Hello, Alice!" */}
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"If the `name` prop is not provided, the component will use the default value "Guest"."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Prop Immutability"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Props are read-only from the perspective of the child component.  A child component should *never* attempt to modify the props it receives.  React enforces this immutability to ensure predictable data flow and prevent unexpected side effects."},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"If a child component needs to modify data, the parent component should manage the state and pass down a handler function as a prop. The child component can then call this function to request a state update in the parent."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"function ParentComponent() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);

  const incrementCount = () => {
    setCount(count + 1);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <ChildComponent onIncrement={incrementCount} />
    </div>
  );
}

function ChildComponent({ onIncrement }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onIncrement}>Increment</button>
  );
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `ChildComponent` receives the `onIncrement` function as a prop. When the button is clicked, it calls `onIncrement`, which updates the `count` state in `ParentComponent`. The `ParentComponent` then re-renders, passing the updated `count` value."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Prop Types (Using PropTypes Library)"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"While not strictly enforced at runtime in modern React (with TypeScript being the preferred solution for type safety), the `prop-types` library provides a way to specify the expected data types for props. This can help catch errors during development."},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, install the `prop-types` package:"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"npm install prop-types"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

function MyComponent({ name, age, isStudent }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Name: {name}</p>
      <p>Age: {age}</p>
      <p>Is Student: {isStudent ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

MyComponent.propTypes = {
  name: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
  age: PropTypes.number,
  isStudent: PropTypes.bool,
};
"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `propTypes` defines that `name` should be a string and is required, `age` should be a number, and `isStudent` should be a boolean. If the props passed to `MyComponent` don't match these types, React will display a warning in the console during development."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep props immutable: Never modify props directly within a child component."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pass data down: Use props to pass data from parent to child components."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use handler functions: Pass handler functions as props to allow child components to trigger state updates in parent components."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider TypeScript: For larger projects, TypeScript provides superior type safety and helps prevent prop-related errors."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Props are a fundamental concept in React. Understanding how to pass data effectively and maintain immutability is crucial for building robust and maintainable React applications. By following these guidelines, you can create components that are easy to reason about and less prone to errors."}]
